-- title:
--   Existence of a canonical L-map for the base-changed module Φ̄
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, $k$ an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$, and $\iota : \mathbb{W}(\mathbb{F}_{p^2}) \to W(k)$ a ring homomorphism; write $\bar\iota$ for its reduction modulo the ideal generated by $p$. Let $\Phi$ be a formal $O_D$-module (a two-dimensional commutative formal group over $W(k)/pW(k)$ with an action of $\mathbb{W}(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})$ and an endomorphism $\varpi$ satisfying $\varpi^2 = [p]$ and $\varpi \circ [a] = [\sigma a] \circ \varpi$) which is special for $\bar\iota$ (its Lie algebra is the direct sum of the invertible eigen-submodules $\mathrm{lieZero}$ and $\mathrm{lieOne}$), has height $4$ (the kernel of $[p]$ has degree $p^4$), satisfies $\mathrm{lieZero}(\bar\iota) \subseteq \ker(\mathrm{lieVarpi})$, and whose Cartier module has complementary graded pieces in degrees $0$ and $1$ for $\bar\iota$; let $r_\Phi : \mathbb{Z}_p^2 \to N$ be an additive map into the $N$-module of the resulting graded Cartier datum which, for every canonical $L$-map $L$ on that datum, maps $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$ bijectively onto the degree-$0$ $\eta$-piece attached to $L$. Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$ that is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra, $\psi' : W(k) \to K$ a ring homomorphism, with $p$ nilpotent in $K$, and let $t'$ be a rigidified object over $K$ (a formal $O_D$-module $X$ over $K$, an integer $n$, and a series $\rho$ over $K/pK$) which is admissible for $(\iota,\psi')$, i.e. $X$ is special of height $4$ and $\rho$ is an isogeny of height $4n$ from $\bar\Phi_{\psi'}$ to $\bar X$. Assume, over the localisation of $K$ at the powers of $1$, that the graded pieces in degrees $0$ and $1$ are complementary for $X$, for its reduction, and for $\bar\Phi_S = (\Phi \otimes K/pK)$ reduced along that localisation, and let $L'$ be a canonical $L$-map for the graded Cartier datum of $X$ over that localisation. Then the graded Cartier datum of $\bar\Phi_S$ with structure map $\mathrm{jPhiS}$ admits a canonical $L$-map, i.e. an additive map $L_\Phi$ from its module to its $N$-module that is Frobenius-semilinear, sends $V x$ to the class of $(\varpi x, 0)$, composes with $\lambda$ to give Frobenius, and descends from a Cartier $L$-map on a special Cartier module over a $p$-torsion-free surjective cover via base change.
--
--   This supplies the auxiliary canonical $L$-map on the Cartier datum of the base point $\Phi$ after base change to $K$, in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation setting of special formal $O_D$-modules of height $4$. It is invoked by the determinant and coordinate computations for rigidification numerators and $\eta$-sections over an algebraically closed base.
